WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:10.020
- Welcome to tonight's panel discussion

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:15.610
election 2020, what
happened and what's next?

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:16.770
I am Alan Gerber.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:17.780
I'm the Director of the

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:20.780
Institution for Social and Policy Studies

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:22.700
and Professor of Political Science.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:24.660
and I'm gonna be serving as

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.923
the moderator of tonight's event.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:31.669
ISPS is the sponsor of tonight's event.

00:00:31.669 --> 00:00:35.060
ISPS is a University Research Center

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:38.080
advancing disciplinary and
interdisciplinary research

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:42.130
in the social sciences
to understand our society

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:44.570
and inform scholarly debate,

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:48.250
democratic deliberation and public policy.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:51.500
The format for tonight's event will be

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:54.040
first comments from each of the panelists.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.463
They're gonna talk for five minutes or so.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:01.100
Following their initial comments,

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.430
we're gonna be having a panel discussion

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:05.290
of maybe 15 or 20 minutes.

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:08.200
We'll see how much time we have for that.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.760
And then we wanna leave a half an hour

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:13.385
for question and answer session

00:01:13.385 --> 00:01:17.150
from answering questions
from the participants.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:20.120
Let me describe some of
the logistics of that.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:24.210
Please send questions
throughout the presentations

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:29.210
using the Q&amp;A button on the
bottom of the Zoom screen.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:32.280
Tonight's event is being recorded.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.253
If you need technical assistance,

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:39.620
please email ISPS@yale.edu.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:41.410
We're gonna be monitoring that

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:43.840
in case you have some
technical difficulties

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:44.933
we can help you with.

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:50.483
Tonight's panelists in
order of appearance,

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:53.300
the way we're gonna do
this as I'm gonna introduce

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:55.960
all five of our distinguished panelists

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:59.860
and then they're gonna just talk in order.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:01.980
So in order of appearance,

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.203
tonight's outstanding panel panelists.

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:07.403
First, we'll be hearing from Jacob Hacker.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:13.040
Jacob is the Stanley Reser
Professor of Political Science

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:18.040
and he was the Director of
ISPS from 2011 until 2020.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:23.690
So he was the director right before me

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:27.223
and I still have a lot
to learn from Jacob.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:29.170
Jacob is a member

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:32.160
of the American Academy
of Arts and Sciences,

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.360
the National Academy of Social Insurance

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:39.360
and the recipient of numerous
awards for his scholarship.

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:42.900
He's especially known for his writings

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.100
on American politics and public policy.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:50.630
And he is an expert on healthcare policy

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:52.740
and he's one of the originators

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:55.600
and developers of the public option

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:00.600
which is really a critical
part of the policy debate

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.020
on healthcare in the United States.

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:05.470
He's the author of numerous books

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:09.430
including a work with his
coauthor, Paul Pierson.

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:13.440
And I see right behind
Jacob's right shoulder

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.160
from his perspective and I
guess left shoulder from ours

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.240
is his most recent book,
"Let Them Eat Tweets."

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:26.240
and so you can help make
that book a bestseller.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:32.160
Our second speaker tonight
will be Christina Kinane.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.740
Professor Kinane is an Assistant Professor

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:38.120
of Political Science at
Yale and a research fellow

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:41.350
at the Institution for
Social and Policy Studies.

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:44.760
Professor Kinane studies
the executive branch

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.950
and political control of the bureaucracy

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:50.140
including the role of
legislatures, executives

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:52.800
and the bureaucracy in policymaking.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.650
Professor Kinane is working
on a book (indistinct)

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:58.977
entitled "Vacancy Politics
Presidential Appointments,

00:03:58.977 --> 00:04:02.510
"and the Strategic
Evasion of Senate Consent"

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:04.080
which investigates how presidents

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.170
strategically use vacancies

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:08.363
to promote their policy priorities.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.550
Next we'll hear from Isabela Mares,

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:16.260
the Arnold Wolfers Professor
of Political Science.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:19.320
Mares studies a range of
topics in comparative politics

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:22.740
and political economy,
including democratization,

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:25.580
clients wisdom and corruption, taxation,

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:30.110
and fiscal capacity development
and social policy reforms

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:32.730
in both developed and
developing countries.

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:35.520
Her recent book co-authored
with Lauren Young

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:39.217
is "Conditionality and
Coercion Electoral Clientelism

00:04:39.217 --> 00:04:40.960
"in Eastern Europe."

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:41.793
And that book won

00:04:41.793 --> 00:04:44.760
the American Political
Science Associations,

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:48.070
extremely prominent and eminent Award,

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:50.470
The William Wright Kerr Award

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:52.653
for best book in political economy.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:56.380
So we'll hear from Isabela.

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:59.340
After Isabela, we get David Mayhew,

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:01.610
again, we're going in alphabetical order.

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:04.140
David Mayhew is Sterling Professor

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:06.400
Emeritus of Political Science,

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:09.540
a fellow of the American
Academy of Arts and Sciences,

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:12.280
the American Philosophical Society

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:14.210
and the National Academy of Sciences.

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:18.530
That's the trifecta, I think,
in the social sciences.

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:20.663
So congratulations to David.

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:26.480
David is a renowned scholar
of American politics

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:29.063
an expert on pretty much everything.

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:34.370
He's the author of perhaps
the most influential book

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:36.707
on Congress of the last 50 years,

00:05:36.707 --> 00:05:39.700
"Congress, the Electoral Connection."

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:41.370
He has a lot of other books,

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:43.463
every one of them is well worth reading.

00:05:44.357 --> 00:05:48.830
"America's Congress" is in
a really nice one, 2000.

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:52.690
I really liked that one and
then "Divided We Govern"

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:56.580
may or may not be the book to turn to

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:59.043
depending how it will go in January.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.170
And finally, we'll be
hearing from Saad Omer.

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:08.940
Professor Omer is a specialist
in infectious diseases

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:11.490
and vaccination policy.

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:16.460
In 2020, I don't know that
there's any post-election panel

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:19.240
that can be formed

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:22.700
without having an expert
on infectious diseases.

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:24.700
Like Saad as part of the discussion

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:28.160
because of the extraordinary importance

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:32.010
of our public health
policy over the last year

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:33.383
and in the coming year.

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:37.050
Professor Omer is a professor of medicine

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:39.100
at the Yale School of Medicine

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:42.700
and the Susan Dwight Bliss,
Professor of Epidemiology

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:45.250
at the Yale School of Public Health.

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:47.953
He's a fellow, oh, excuse me,

00:06:47.953 --> 00:06:51.890
he's the director of the Yale
Institute for Global Health

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:55.570
and a fellow of the Infectious
Diseases Society of America

00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:58.190
winner of awards for his scholarship

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:02.010
on the impact of immunizations.

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:05.410
He conducts research on interventions

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:08.910
to increase immunization
coverage and acceptance.

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:12.060
Again incredibly critical
public policy issue

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:13.333
in the coming months.

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:17.920
Professor Omer served
as an academic affiliate

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:20.780
of the White House Social
and Behavioral Sciences team

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:23.550
under, I guess it was Barack Obama

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:26.310
and he is on the advisory panels

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:30.750
for the important vaccination bench

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:34.160
trying to understand what
policies need to be set

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:38.290
regarding the vaccination
approvals and distribution.

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:42.130
He is on both the United
States Advisory Panel

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:47.280
for the vaccines, as
well as the WHO Panel.

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:52.280
So he is a real expert on this

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:54.813
incredibly important public policy issue.

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:59.423
Welcome to all of our panelists.

00:08:00.410 --> 00:08:02.550
One last thing before we turn it over

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:04.253
to these distinguished panelists,

00:08:05.802 --> 00:08:10.330
if you enjoy today's event, there's more.

00:08:10.330 --> 00:08:15.330
We're having another event
December the 1st at 7:30 to 8:45.

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:21.770
Tonight's panelists and our discussion

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:25.960
will be focused to some
extent a bit more on

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:30.960
the macro picture public
policy of governance.

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:33.160
On the 1st of December,

00:08:33.160 --> 00:08:37.970
we have more of a political
behavior heavy panel.

00:08:37.970 --> 00:08:41.530
So if you wanna know things
about what the voters did

00:08:41.530 --> 00:08:43.380
and why did they do it,

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:45.680
you'll get some of that
tonight, I would imagine.

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:49.990
But you'll get a lot of
it on December 1st at 7:30

00:08:49.990 --> 00:08:54.370
for that panel, we'll be having
Alex Coppock and Josh Kalla

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:56.350
from the Yale Political
Science Department,

00:08:56.350 --> 00:08:58.500
two political behavior scholars

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:01.780
as well as Mike Podhorzer
who is the assistant

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:05.793
to the president of the
AFL-CIO for strategic research.

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:10.690
Mike is a incredible
expert on the nitty gritty

00:09:10.690 --> 00:09:14.830
of the American voter and
American political behavior

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:18.800
especially contemporary
American political behavior.

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:20.980
We'll also be having Lynn Vavreck

00:09:20.980 --> 00:09:23.250
who has been conducting
a tremendous amount

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:26.920
of survey research during the campaign.

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:29.300
And she's an expert on
presidential elections

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:32.370
and campaign effects in
presidential elections.

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:34.570
So if you're interested in that angle,

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:39.200
the political behavior angle
on December 1st at 7:30,

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:42.600
please look out for the ISPS events,

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:46.040
what did voters do and why did they do it.

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:49.680
With that, let me turn
it over to our panelists

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:53.530
who will speak without any
intervening interruption

00:09:53.530 --> 00:09:56.110
in alphabetical order.

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:58.303
So please kick us off Jacob.

00:09:59.870 --> 00:10:00.770
- Great (indistinct).

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:03.780
Thanks so much, Alan,

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:07.360
thanks to Lamar Pier
and Pam Greene for doing

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:11.980
the backside tech and
preparation for this event.

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:14.840
And thanks to this amazing
group of panelists.

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:17.870
It's a real pleasure to be with you.

00:10:17.870 --> 00:10:20.780
I also appreciate Alan's
mentioning the book,

00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:23.130
I see we have about 245 attendees

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:24.870
so you could double my book sales

00:10:24.870 --> 00:10:27.023
if you all went on and bought the book.

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:29.630
Well, I think we all should celebrate.

00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:31.570
It's now official.

00:10:31.570 --> 00:10:34.790
Charles Koch has said that
the winner is Joe Biden.

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:37.373
So we should just all be clapping.

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:40.800
For those who didn't read the interview

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:43.730
with the elder Koch brother,

00:10:43.730 --> 00:10:46.290
the surviving of the
two that are associated

00:10:46.290 --> 00:10:48.650
with conservative political causes.

00:10:48.650 --> 00:10:49.650
He also went on to say

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:51.270
that he was really sorry that he messed up

00:10:51.270 --> 00:10:54.500
American politics with all his investments

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:59.253
to which the Twitterati responded

00:10:59.253 --> 00:11:01.833
with a mix of humor and contempt.

00:11:02.710 --> 00:11:06.610
Here's my favorite, he said
something to the effect of

00:11:06.610 --> 00:11:08.790
we really screwed things
up is what Koch said.

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:10.440
This is the response.

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:12.350
You can screw up a cookie recipe,

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:14.360
you can screw up parallel parking,

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:16.250
those things can be
shrugged off and repaired.

00:11:16.250 --> 00:11:18.560
You can't screw up a
country, its elections,

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:21.040
its media landscape, a
major political party

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:23.330
and just say, oops, my bad.

00:11:23.330 --> 00:11:25.730
So I wanna talk a little bit about

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:28.890
that major political party because

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:31.010
I think we should first recognize

00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:34.500
that the electoral defeat
of a would be authoritarian

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:36.290
is a very big deal.

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:41.290
I think Joe Biden would add
another word to the description

00:11:41.900 --> 00:11:45.363
between big and deal but this
is a major accomplishment,

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:48.360
but I think as Paul and I'd argue,

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:49.967
Paul Pierson and I argued in our book,

00:11:49.967 --> 00:11:53.340
"Let Them Eat Tweets,"
which begins with the line

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:56.330
this is not a book about Donald Trump.

00:11:56.330 --> 00:11:59.250
Donald Trump is not the sole reason

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:03.190
as Charles Koch's comments suggest

00:12:03.190 --> 00:12:05.560
that our democracy has not been working

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:07.650
as well as it could or should.

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:11.300
And I think the election
delivered a kind of split verdict

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:14.720
on the future of American democracy.

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:17.113
On the one hand, as I said,

00:12:18.040 --> 00:12:21.170
would be authoritarians are
rarely defeated at the polls

00:12:21.170 --> 00:12:24.560
and democratic backsliding
as the comparative college

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:27.110
could've continued much more quickly

00:12:27.110 --> 00:12:29.360
had Donald Trump been reelected.

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:30.740
But at the same time,

00:12:30.740 --> 00:12:34.890
the fact that Republicans
actually gained seats in the house

00:12:35.770 --> 00:12:37.570
may well hold on to the Senate

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:39.390
And it seems like the more likely outcome,

00:12:39.390 --> 00:12:43.490
I'm sure David will give us
examples of every runoff race

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:47.260
in the history of the United States

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:49.183
to analyze the prospects.

00:12:50.060 --> 00:12:52.950
But it seems likely that
Republicans will hold the Senate

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:55.870
and thus that Mitch McConnell
will be the majority leader.

00:12:55.870 --> 00:13:00.870
And that is obviously gonna mean that

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:05.680
the president elect's ability
to achieve many of the goals

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:07.580
he set out during the
campaign will be limited.

00:13:07.580 --> 00:13:11.630
It also means, and I think
this is really important

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:13.290
to understand is that

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:16.140
the pressure is on the Republican
party to change course.

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:19.450
They're gonna be less than
they might've been otherwise.

00:13:19.450 --> 00:13:24.450
Back in the late, well
actually the early 2010s,

00:13:24.530 --> 00:13:28.430
Barack Obama famously asked
whether the GOP fever will break

00:13:28.430 --> 00:13:32.313
and that fever unfortunately
still seems to be raging.

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:37.550
The other thing I wanna just mention

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:39.290
and I think we should talk about tonight

00:13:39.290 --> 00:13:44.210
is the extent to which this
party that has been radicalized

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:47.372
over the last generation has
managed to insulate itself

00:13:47.372 --> 00:13:52.372
from electoral
accountability in many ways.

00:13:53.830 --> 00:13:57.950
The fact is that
Republicans have brode into

00:13:57.950 --> 00:14:01.140
America's counter
majoritarian institution.

00:14:01.140 --> 00:14:06.030
They hold their appointees
hold a decisive majority

00:14:06.030 --> 00:14:08.390
on the Supreme Court and lower courts.

00:14:08.390 --> 00:14:09.329
It's a notable fact

00:14:09.329 --> 00:14:13.620
that the 63 majority
on the Supreme Court is

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:16.820
a good chunk of the
people who constitute it

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:20.910
were either or both
appointed by a president

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:23.350
who did not win the popular vote

00:14:23.350 --> 00:14:26.830
or confirmed by a Senate majority

00:14:26.830 --> 00:14:29.140
that did not represent
a majority of Americans.

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:30.170
And we know that the Senate

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:33.050
is heavily tilted towards rural America

00:14:33.050 --> 00:14:34.820
and that rural America is heavily tilted

00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:36.690
toward the Republican party.

00:14:36.690 --> 00:14:38.500
And we also know that
the Electoral College

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:43.500
has become a much advantageous
terrain for the Republicans.

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:46.950
So there's a variety of
reasons why Republicans

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:49.020
are under less pressure to moderate

00:14:49.990 --> 00:14:53.740
even though the most extreme instantiation

00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:55.530
of the Republican retreat

00:14:57.140 --> 00:15:00.390
from the center Donald Trump was defeated.

00:15:00.390 --> 00:15:02.570
So I'll just close in my last few minutes

00:15:02.570 --> 00:15:07.040
by mentioning what I
consider to be the kind of

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:09.780
a half full or half empty version

00:15:09.780 --> 00:15:12.210
of our democratic glass right now.

00:15:12.210 --> 00:15:14.380
So on the one hand, as I said,

00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:18.640
I think we've seen a clear
rebuke to a president who

00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:25.070
has continued to flout democratic
norms and who, as I said,

00:15:25.220 --> 00:15:27.050
represented a clear and present danger

00:15:27.050 --> 00:15:28.623
to American democracy.

00:15:29.770 --> 00:15:33.960
On, the other hand, the fact
that we have Republican silence

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:37.050
or worse in the face of a failed president

00:15:37.050 --> 00:15:39.690
continuing to do legitimated elections

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:43.920
is an extremely worrisome
sign that we're going to see

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:45.870
continuing intense fights

00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:49.490
over the future of American
democracy and American politics.

00:15:49.490 --> 00:15:53.510
The base of the Republican
party is thoroughly Trumpian.

00:15:53.510 --> 00:15:55.470
The structures of organized outrage

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:58.580
that have supported him remain in place.

00:15:58.580 --> 00:16:01.390
Indeed Fox News now looks
like a moderate alternative

00:16:01.390 --> 00:16:04.820
to other sources of
information for conservatives.

00:16:04.820 --> 00:16:07.940
And finally we know from history that

00:16:07.940 --> 00:16:10.810
obstruction has paid off
for the Republican party.

00:16:10.810 --> 00:16:15.280
Mitch McConnell, once again
will be in the position of

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:18.520
deciding what is possible for an incoming

00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:19.810
democratic administration.

00:16:19.810 --> 00:16:21.430
And will almost certainly say,

00:16:21.430 --> 00:16:24.280
as he did back under President Obama

00:16:24.280 --> 00:16:27.780
that his top priority is to
make the democratic incumbent

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:29.230
a one-term president.

00:16:29.230 --> 00:16:31.410
How will it end up, let's stay tuned

00:16:31.410 --> 00:16:32.860
and let's discuss it tonight.

00:16:40.450 --> 00:16:42.227
- Okay well thank you Jacob.

00:16:42.227 --> 00:16:45.460
And thank you, Alan and Pam and Omer

00:16:45.460 --> 00:16:47.820
for all of your organization efforts.

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:50.300
I am thrilled to be here
to be a part of this

00:16:51.490 --> 00:16:55.083
incredibly amazing panel

00:16:55.083 --> 00:16:59.030
and to kind of discuss and digest

00:16:59.030 --> 00:17:02.080
a bit of what we've
experienced politically

00:17:02.080 --> 00:17:04.800
for the last year of the election,

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:06.790
for the last four years of
this current administration

00:17:06.790 --> 00:17:07.990
to kind of look forward.

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:10.970
The topic of this panel being

00:17:12.290 --> 00:17:15.030
what happened and what's next.

00:17:15.030 --> 00:17:20.030
Jacob, you lead us off with
such a wonderful set of thoughts

00:17:20.170 --> 00:17:23.740
on how we might consider moving forward

00:17:23.740 --> 00:17:25.220
with the Republican party as it is.

00:17:25.220 --> 00:17:28.730
I'd like to add a little bit
of my own flavor to that mix

00:17:28.730 --> 00:17:33.410
and think through or at
least start the conversation

00:17:33.410 --> 00:17:36.860
on one of the pillars of the
Biden campaign really ran on

00:17:36.860 --> 00:17:39.330
which was the administration's handling

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:44.330
or some might say mishandling
of the COVID-19 epidemic.

00:17:45.787 --> 00:17:47.940
And what that really
shines a bright light on

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:52.940
is the in which the administration
has handled appointments

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:55.590
to the federal bureaucracy

00:17:55.590 --> 00:17:59.890
and the way that the
administration has ran and managed

00:17:59.890 --> 00:18:04.890
our governments or
mismanaged as some might say.

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:07.820
And particularly thinking
through exactly what happened

00:18:07.820 --> 00:18:09.450
in the last four years,

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:11.230
the Trump administration is leaving us

00:18:11.230 --> 00:18:15.220
with a federal government
that is led by mostly

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:18.350
acting officials in these oftentimes are

00:18:18.350 --> 00:18:20.010
or not oftentimes rather,

00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:23.960
almost entirely not vetted by the Senate.

00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:25.610
They're not confirmed by the Senate,

00:18:25.610 --> 00:18:29.210
they're filling those positions
on an interim capacity.

00:18:29.210 --> 00:18:31.850
But under the Federal
Vacancies Reform Act,

00:18:31.850 --> 00:18:33.990
they're only supposed to serve
for a limited amount of time

00:18:33.990 --> 00:18:37.190
and Trump is exceedingly

00:18:37.190 --> 00:18:39.630
either just blown past those deadlines

00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:42.940
or submitted nominees to a Senate who

00:18:42.940 --> 00:18:47.500
as Jacob so nicely put
is run by Republicans

00:18:47.500 --> 00:18:50.340
and you would think would
then confirm his appointees

00:18:50.340 --> 00:18:53.710
that aren't, they aren't
confirming his appointees.

00:18:53.710 --> 00:18:56.380
This leaves these actings
in those positions.

00:18:56.380 --> 00:18:58.800
We've seen a variety of things come down

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:03.391
where those actings are
potentially not legal,

00:19:03.391 --> 00:19:06.030
the decisions that they make
are not legal most recently

00:19:06.030 --> 00:19:10.210
with acting Secretary of
Homeland Security, Chad Wolf.

00:19:10.210 --> 00:19:12.100
And the question this brings

00:19:12.100 --> 00:19:14.100
is what the Biden administration can do

00:19:14.100 --> 00:19:15.780
in the process of transitioning.

00:19:15.780 --> 00:19:18.480
When you transition to
a new administration,

00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:20.100
you're reliance on the individuals

00:19:20.100 --> 00:19:22.920
who are filling the
positions at this time.

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:25.840
And oftentimes in that transition,

00:19:25.840 --> 00:19:28.747
you'll see the top
cabinet positions set off

00:19:28.747 --> 00:19:32.230
and the deputy secretaries
takeover for short amount of time

00:19:32.230 --> 00:19:35.030
to kind of fill the gaps as an acting.

00:19:35.030 --> 00:19:37.120
But when you have acting
deputy secretaries

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:38.110
or you have vacancies

00:19:38.110 --> 00:19:40.310
in those acting deputy
secretary positions,

00:19:40.310 --> 00:19:42.580
so that someone can move up

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:45.200
into the acting secretary position itself.

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:46.570
This calls into question

00:19:46.570 --> 00:19:48.160
exactly how the Biden administration

00:19:48.160 --> 00:19:49.450
is gonna get their feet under them

00:19:49.450 --> 00:19:52.124
and get off onto a good start.

00:19:52.124 --> 00:19:56.000
And really when our conversation
turns to what's next,

00:19:56.000 --> 00:19:57.530
that's what we have to
start thinking about

00:19:57.530 --> 00:19:59.380
with the Biden administration

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:01.350
transitioning out of this election

00:20:01.350 --> 00:20:03.390
and thinking about the
hurdles that already exist

00:20:03.390 --> 00:20:05.490
to a new administration.

00:20:05.490 --> 00:20:08.560
You have 1200 presidential appointments

00:20:08.560 --> 00:20:11.270
that require Senate
confirmation to be filled.

00:20:11.270 --> 00:20:13.110
Now we can have a
conversation at a later time

00:20:13.110 --> 00:20:14.780
as to whether or not that is necessary.

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:17.690
The politicization of the
federal government certainly

00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:21.560
is questionable for a lot
and has been a subject

00:20:21.560 --> 00:20:24.160
of research for political scientists.

00:20:24.160 --> 00:20:26.930
But when we think as to what Biden's

00:20:26.930 --> 00:20:29.950
gonna be able to achieve
as Jacob mentioned,

00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:32.090
he's going to need to
rely on those appointees.

00:20:32.090 --> 00:20:36.740
And if he is stymied by the
Senate in terms of confirmation,

00:20:36.740 --> 00:20:39.870
Trump has really laid out
there almost a playbook

00:20:39.870 --> 00:20:43.320
for pursuing his own
strategies of appointments

00:20:43.320 --> 00:20:44.800
outside of the Senate.

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:47.980
So I'm excited to kind of think
through what means as well

00:20:47.980 --> 00:20:50.803
with the rest of the panelists, thank you.

00:20:54.210 --> 00:20:56.300
- Okay, thank you so much to Alan

00:20:56.300 --> 00:20:58.990
for inviting me to be part of this panel

00:20:58.990 --> 00:21:02.780
and to Pam and Omer for organizing.

00:21:02.780 --> 00:21:07.700
So, as Alan mentioned, I am
studying comparative politics.

00:21:07.700 --> 00:21:09.984
And so this identity
in fact helps me today

00:21:09.984 --> 00:21:12.277
because quite honestly,
there's so much about

00:21:12.277 --> 00:21:15.380
the 2020 elections that
I don't understand.

00:21:15.380 --> 00:21:16.213
Okay (laughs).

00:21:17.570 --> 00:21:19.350
So at the time Alan has asked me

00:21:19.350 --> 00:21:20.640
to be part of this round table,

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:23.170
I wouldn't have anticipated
that we would be today

00:21:23.170 --> 00:21:24.890
nearly two weeks after the election

00:21:24.890 --> 00:21:27.673
in this ambiguous situation,
which is a slow motion.

00:21:29.000 --> 00:21:32.070
So the outgoing president
has not yet recognized

00:21:32.070 --> 00:21:33.530
the legitimacy of the election

00:21:33.530 --> 00:21:35.980
nor has he yet considered defeat.

00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:39.020
While perhaps this is
unsurprising for Donald Trump,

00:21:39.020 --> 00:21:41.340
what is more worrying
about democratic erosion

00:21:41.340 --> 00:21:45.310
is that the Republican party
is complicit with the strategy.

00:21:45.310 --> 00:21:48.950
Nearly half of the political
class elected to the Senate

00:21:48.950 --> 00:21:51.800
and to the house today are
complicit in a strategy

00:21:51.800 --> 00:21:55.210
to delegitimize a democratically
elected president.

00:21:55.210 --> 00:21:58.320
Yesterday, The Washington Post
reported based on interviews

00:21:58.320 --> 00:22:00.500
of the Georgia secretary of state

00:22:00.500 --> 00:22:03.660
that Senator Lindsey Graham
tried to pressure him

00:22:03.660 --> 00:22:04.890
that the Georgia Secretary of State

00:22:04.890 --> 00:22:07.013
to find ways to exclude legal balance.

00:22:08.120 --> 00:22:09.790
So how does this moment in time,

00:22:09.790 --> 00:22:12.650
how does this unprecedented
democratic fragility

00:22:12.650 --> 00:22:15.800
of the United States fit
in the democratic landscape

00:22:15.800 --> 00:22:18.180
of eroding democracies?

00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:20.240
So I want to draw
attention to the fact that

00:22:20.240 --> 00:22:22.730
if we look in a comparative setting,

00:22:22.730 --> 00:22:25.430
the situation in the
United States is bleak.

00:22:25.430 --> 00:22:27.650
So hearing about this episode yesterday,

00:22:27.650 --> 00:22:31.367
I was reminded about Nancy
Bermeo's in very important book

00:22:31.367 --> 00:22:34.340
"Ordinary People In Extraordinary Times".

00:22:34.340 --> 00:22:35.780
I don't know how many of you here

00:22:35.780 --> 00:22:37.930
are familiar with this
study but to my knowledge,

00:22:37.930 --> 00:22:39.890
this is the best account
of democratic erosion

00:22:39.890 --> 00:22:41.940
we have in comparative politics.

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:43.740
This is a study of 18 countries

00:22:43.740 --> 00:22:45.460
that experienced democratic breakdown

00:22:45.460 --> 00:22:47.280
in Europe and Latin America

00:22:47.280 --> 00:22:50.625
during the interwar period
in the '60s and '70s.

00:22:50.625 --> 00:22:52.470
Bermeo asks the simple question.

00:22:52.470 --> 00:22:55.470
What distinguishes cases
that remain democracies

00:22:55.470 --> 00:22:58.760
from cases where democracy
ultimately caught that.

00:22:58.760 --> 00:23:00.810
Her main result is highly relevant

00:23:00.810 --> 00:23:03.710
for the situation of
the United States today.

00:23:03.710 --> 00:23:07.180
The studies on the
importance of elite behavior

00:23:07.180 --> 00:23:11.270
and on a variable that Bermeo
calls distancing capacity.

00:23:11.270 --> 00:23:13.300
She defines distancing capacity

00:23:13.300 --> 00:23:16.110
as the ability of members
of the incumbent party

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:18.935
to distance themselves from the behavior,

00:23:18.935 --> 00:23:22.560
from the lawless behavior
of their co-partizan allies

00:23:23.420 --> 00:23:25.090
end of the quote.

00:23:25.090 --> 00:23:27.160
So evidence of such distancing capacity

00:23:27.160 --> 00:23:29.400
comes in the form of
condemnation of lawlessness

00:23:29.400 --> 00:23:30.680
of the prosecution.

00:23:30.680 --> 00:23:33.090
This was present in Finland
and the Czech Republic

00:23:33.090 --> 00:23:36.010
and then over a period and
this was where these members

00:23:36.010 --> 00:23:38.190
of the dominant sanction violence

00:23:38.190 --> 00:23:40.450
or transgressions of democratic norms,

00:23:40.450 --> 00:23:41.528
but was absent in countries that

00:23:41.528 --> 00:23:44.493
sort of right where democracy collapsed.

00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:48.940
So what is remarkable about
the United States today

00:23:48.940 --> 00:23:52.270
in broad comparison to
these historical cases

00:23:52.270 --> 00:23:54.640
is that it takes extremely low measures

00:23:54.640 --> 00:23:57.630
on the variable measuring
distancing capacity.

00:23:57.630 --> 00:24:00.950
Moreover, I cannot see a
way how distancing capacity

00:24:00.950 --> 00:24:03.550
can be established within
the Republican party.

00:24:03.550 --> 00:24:06.360
Who the actors of change
are and what sanctions

00:24:06.360 --> 00:24:09.510
they may have at their disposal
to enforce this behavior

00:24:09.510 --> 00:24:11.610
among their co-partisans.

00:24:11.610 --> 00:24:13.830
So at this critical historical juncture,

00:24:13.830 --> 00:24:16.290
Republicans are complicit
with Trump's decision

00:24:16.290 --> 00:24:18.120
to deny the legitimacy of the election,

00:24:18.120 --> 00:24:20.470
just to increase their bargaining power

00:24:20.470 --> 00:24:21.980
with a new president.

00:24:21.980 --> 00:24:24.990
So this is my view creates
a very somber scenario

00:24:24.990 --> 00:24:26.200
for the Biden's presidency

00:24:26.200 --> 00:24:28.670
who would have to devote
considerable energy

00:24:28.670 --> 00:24:30.560
to fight off accusation of illegitimacy

00:24:30.560 --> 00:24:35.080
in the eyes of sort of
about 30% of the voters.

00:24:35.080 --> 00:24:38.150
So the future in my view is oblique.

00:24:38.150 --> 00:24:40.540
So turning on to the elections,

00:24:40.540 --> 00:24:42.920
I have sort of one or two kind of thoughts

00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:44.920
about the outcome of the election

00:24:44.920 --> 00:24:48.010
and they have to do with the
nature of popular appeals

00:24:48.010 --> 00:24:50.410
The elections have build
a very ambiguous results

00:24:50.410 --> 00:24:53.440
that I find quite difficult to understand.

00:24:53.440 --> 00:24:55.910
So even though President
Trump lost the election,

00:24:55.910 --> 00:24:59.060
over 70 million voters voted for him.

00:24:59.060 --> 00:25:02.220
This can see in the middle
of a precedent pandemic

00:25:02.220 --> 00:25:05.540
and the disastrous response
of the federal government

00:25:05.540 --> 00:25:07.650
to this public health crisis.

00:25:07.650 --> 00:25:10.180
So this is really positive
for most explanations

00:25:10.180 --> 00:25:11.800
in political science that highlight

00:25:11.800 --> 00:25:14.890
the ability of voters to
sanction bad politicians

00:25:14.890 --> 00:25:17.083
especially in moments of economic crisis.

00:25:18.290 --> 00:25:20.540
At the same time, the election has yielded

00:25:20.540 --> 00:25:23.150
very ambiguous results for Democrats.

00:25:23.150 --> 00:25:25.517
Even if President Biden
won the popular vote,

00:25:25.517 --> 00:25:27.430
the election has not been successful

00:25:27.430 --> 00:25:30.090
by the most Democratic
candidates on ballot.

00:25:30.090 --> 00:25:32.470
The Democratic majority
in the house has shrunk

00:25:34.845 --> 00:25:36.580
and no Democrat competing in
Red States has been reelected.

00:25:36.580 --> 00:25:38.850
So what explanations do we
have in political science

00:25:38.850 --> 00:25:41.100
to make sense of these results?

00:25:41.100 --> 00:25:42.050
This suggests to me

00:25:42.050 --> 00:25:43.036
that we have really not fully appreciated

00:25:43.036 --> 00:25:46.770
the power and strength
of populist appears.

00:25:46.770 --> 00:25:48.590
So what do I mean by populist?

00:25:48.590 --> 00:25:50.480
So even though we have
experienced four years

00:25:50.480 --> 00:25:51.900
of a populist president,

00:25:51.900 --> 00:25:54.580
it's quite hard to explain what they do.

00:25:54.580 --> 00:25:56.450
They govern through provocation,

00:25:56.450 --> 00:25:58.440
they govern through the creation of enemy,

00:25:58.440 --> 00:26:01.310
the fake media, the quote
unquote rapist migrants,

00:26:01.310 --> 00:26:03.700
the quote unquote Chinese virus.

00:26:03.700 --> 00:26:06.340
What populists do is that
they introduce in a policy

00:26:06.340 --> 00:26:09.333
this course, a new dimension
that this orthogonal

00:26:09.333 --> 00:26:11.250
to the existing policy dimension.

00:26:11.250 --> 00:26:12.960
And so they govern by flaming up

00:26:12.960 --> 00:26:14.627
the structuring of opposition they need.

00:26:14.627 --> 00:26:17.809
And so this is what we
call in political science,

00:26:17.809 --> 00:26:20.813
(indistinct) sort of strategies.

00:26:21.790 --> 00:26:23.550
So we don't have a good explanation

00:26:23.550 --> 00:26:26.410
of how these messages work
and we often dismiss them

00:26:26.410 --> 00:26:28.873
as being kind of theatrical of a populist.

00:26:29.880 --> 00:26:32.160
But I think we don't fully understand how

00:26:32.160 --> 00:26:35.080
this populist rhetoric was successful

00:26:35.080 --> 00:26:38.000
to maintain the electoral
coalition of Trump

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:40.963
in the face of the dramatic
economic shock of the pandemic.

00:26:41.960 --> 00:26:44.150
So let me again turn
into the pandemic, right?

00:26:44.150 --> 00:26:46.123
How did Trump respond to the pandemic?

00:26:46.123 --> 00:26:48.810
Like other populists around the world,

00:26:48.810 --> 00:26:50.223
he had two strategies,

00:26:50.223 --> 00:26:54.010
anti-scientists, deny the
existence of the virus

00:26:54.010 --> 00:26:56.000
and sort of the anti-foreign strategies

00:26:56.000 --> 00:26:58.783
blaming foreign countries
such as China on the virus.

00:27:00.450 --> 00:27:03.870
So to understand sort of
like how these messages

00:27:03.870 --> 00:27:07.290
sort of might have helped
him maintain support

00:27:07.290 --> 00:27:10.130
among sort of like his 70 million voters

00:27:10.130 --> 00:27:11.520
which I mentioned above,

00:27:11.520 --> 00:27:12.890
It's important to think about sort of like

00:27:12.890 --> 00:27:15.350
the two effects of sort of like populism.

00:27:15.350 --> 00:27:17.870
One is that this orthogonal dimension

00:27:17.870 --> 00:27:20.920
of political competition creates
a really difficult choice

00:27:20.920 --> 00:27:24.620
for other parties, whether or
not to engage with this issue

00:27:24.620 --> 00:27:26.300
should the Democrats have responded

00:27:26.300 --> 00:27:31.040
to all this nonsense about
Chinese origin of the virus?

00:27:31.040 --> 00:27:33.950
But I do think the more
important consequence

00:27:33.950 --> 00:27:37.190
of these messages is that
they have spill over effects

00:27:37.190 --> 00:27:39.620
on the main access of
political competition,

00:27:39.620 --> 00:27:41.190
the left-right dimension.

00:27:41.190 --> 00:27:43.343
So in other words, what
populous messages do

00:27:43.343 --> 00:27:46.840
is that they depress
the support for policy

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:49.740
or the demand for policy
even among the groups

00:27:49.740 --> 00:27:53.300
that are very severely hurt such as voters

00:27:53.300 --> 00:27:56.620
who have experienced COVID
or have experienced downturn.

00:27:56.620 --> 00:28:00.440
And we have lots of surveys
evidence that I have collected

00:28:00.440 --> 00:28:02.140
with colleagues during the pandemic

00:28:02.140 --> 00:28:03.910
sort of by documenting this.

00:28:03.910 --> 00:28:05.380
That calling sort of rights...

00:28:05.380 --> 00:28:07.510
If you exposed to voters to these messages

00:28:07.510 --> 00:28:08.810
of the virus is a hoax,

00:28:08.810 --> 00:28:10.970
that in fact depresses their demand

00:28:11.862 --> 00:28:15.830
for a policy voters that have
experienced an income shock.

00:28:15.830 --> 00:28:18.070
So I do think this is sort of right part

00:28:18.070 --> 00:28:20.130
of the explanation of the the spousal

00:28:20.130 --> 00:28:24.585
why Trump was able to maintain
electoral coalition intact

00:28:24.585 --> 00:28:28.700
