legislative program presidency.

00:41:17.120 --> 00:41:19.163
It's a very tough four years coming up.

00:41:21.500 --> 00:41:22.430
- Sure, terrific.

00:41:22.430 --> 00:41:23.280
Thank you.

00:41:23.280 --> 00:41:25.040
Saad Omer.

00:41:25.040 --> 00:41:25.873
- So hi.

00:41:26.960 --> 00:41:30.740
You might be wondering what an
epidemiologist is doing here

00:41:30.740 --> 00:41:32.930
and there's one explanation.

00:41:32.930 --> 00:41:37.930
Since everyone went home on March 15th,

00:41:38.530 --> 00:41:42.416
there's an oversupply of
armchair epidemiologists.

00:41:42.416 --> 00:41:47.416
so maybe I'm taking
revenge on other fields

00:41:48.080 --> 00:41:52.050
by becoming an armchair
political scientist

00:41:52.050 --> 00:41:56.880
in this post election season
now, but that's not the reason.

00:41:56.880 --> 00:42:00.550
The reason I'm here is because of

00:42:01.650 --> 00:42:06.650
this pandemic response in
the US has been a failure.

00:42:06.750 --> 00:42:09.160
There's no other way to describe it.

00:42:09.160 --> 00:42:13.650
Those of us who have been
working on infectious diseases

00:42:13.650 --> 00:42:15.870
including pandemic preparedness,

00:42:15.870 --> 00:42:18.380
my first interaction and
my first serious effort

00:42:18.380 --> 00:42:23.380
was actually around
2006 when H5N1 influenza

00:42:24.913 --> 00:42:27.640
was a risk and so on and so forth

00:42:27.640 --> 00:42:31.000
and was engaged with
some work around H1N1.

00:42:31.000 --> 00:42:34.920
And a lot of people had expected that

00:42:34.920 --> 00:42:37.060
there could be a virus

00:42:37.060 --> 00:42:42.060
which would impact the US in
the way it is impacting us now

00:42:43.630 --> 00:42:46.360
which would lead to
deaths even more deaths

00:42:46.360 --> 00:42:49.380
or could lead to deaths in higher numbers.

00:42:49.380 --> 00:42:54.380
But no serious scholar had
imagined and I've asked around.

00:42:55.350 --> 00:42:58.450
I have asked people that if we were...

00:42:58.450 --> 00:43:00.480
We always thought if you were this bad

00:43:00.480 --> 00:43:03.200
in terms of mortality and morbidity,

00:43:03.200 --> 00:43:05.350
not necessarily spread but
specifically mortality,

00:43:05.350 --> 00:43:09.460
there will be a hundred other
countries worse than us.

00:43:09.460 --> 00:43:13.070
And there was a for example,

00:43:13.070 --> 00:43:16.220
the latest global health security index

00:43:16.220 --> 00:43:21.220
ranked which came out in 2019
ranks the US as number one.

00:43:23.280 --> 00:43:25.940
And obviously we are not number one

00:43:25.940 --> 00:43:28.000
at least not in a positive sense.

00:43:28.000 --> 00:43:29.670
And the reason why I'm here is that

00:43:29.670 --> 00:43:31.370
it's not a failure of science.

00:43:31.370 --> 00:43:33.590
We have had PCR and antigen tests

00:43:33.590 --> 00:43:36.900
and social distancing
and masks and vaccines

00:43:36.900 --> 00:43:39.180
although these are
newer types of vaccines,

00:43:39.180 --> 00:43:42.530
but the overall vaccine
approach for decades.

00:43:42.530 --> 00:43:46.440
What failed wasn't science,
what failed was governance.

00:43:46.440 --> 00:43:48.520
And in Public Health,

00:43:48.520 --> 00:43:52.580
if I want to prepare
for the next pandemic,

00:43:52.580 --> 00:43:56.210
I can't do that without an approach

00:43:56.210 --> 00:44:01.210
that involves a serious mature
hard-nosed look at governance

00:44:02.070 --> 00:44:05.860
and governance you know, it is sad.

00:44:05.860 --> 00:44:08.400
I know, I'm sure a lot
of you are aware that

00:44:09.810 --> 00:44:12.043
democracies don't have famines.

00:44:13.310 --> 00:44:17.363
I'm paraphrasing someone obviously.

00:44:18.210 --> 00:44:20.720
But democracies have pandemics and

00:44:22.160 --> 00:44:27.160
democracies make a mess it
seems of pandemic response.

00:44:27.580 --> 00:44:31.000
So that's the reason why
we need to discuss this.

00:44:31.000 --> 00:44:33.130
That's why institutions are important.

00:44:33.130 --> 00:44:35.270
That's why appointments are important.

00:44:35.270 --> 00:44:37.760
That's why prominence of career folks

00:44:37.760 --> 00:44:40.600
and political appointees in decisions

00:44:40.600 --> 00:44:45.600
and random radiologists
from Stanford is important.

00:44:45.700 --> 00:44:50.700
And so one of the things going
forward would be important

00:44:50.740 --> 00:44:52.598
is trust in institutions.

00:44:52.598 --> 00:44:57.598
And part of my work is
focused on vaccine acceptance

00:44:57.670 --> 00:44:59.196
and trust in vaccines.

00:44:59.196 --> 00:45:04.196
And a lot of people that you
have seen different services,

00:45:04.590 --> 00:45:07.090
when we looked at it, it was in may.

00:45:07.090 --> 00:45:08.970
We found only 67% would

00:45:10.110 --> 00:45:11.770
sort of right away accept the vaccine.

00:45:11.770 --> 00:45:14.120
The rates for two other
service pure and others

00:45:14.120 --> 00:45:17.510
have actually gone down
of our current acceptance.

00:45:17.510 --> 00:45:21.410
And these are not your run of
the mild anti-vaccine folks.

00:45:21.410 --> 00:45:23.170
There's a huge chunk of folks

00:45:25.024 --> 00:45:27.680
who are suspicious of the process,

00:45:27.680 --> 00:45:30.700
who saw what happened
with hydroxychloroquine,

00:45:30.700 --> 00:45:32.880
who saw what happened
with convalescent plasma

00:45:32.880 --> 00:45:35.540
where there was perhaps a reason

00:45:35.540 --> 00:45:38.820
to issue emergency authorization.

00:45:38.820 --> 00:45:41.690
And you could argue have a
good faith argument about

00:45:41.690 --> 00:45:43.640
the merits or demerits of that.

00:45:43.640 --> 00:45:45.520
But the way it was projected,

00:45:45.520 --> 00:45:48.590
the way first politicized
freaked a lot of people out.

00:45:48.590 --> 00:45:51.900
And since then, there has been
a cost correction at the FDA

00:45:51.900 --> 00:45:54.910
due to pushback of the career folks

00:45:54.910 --> 00:45:59.910
but also due to pushback
of the political appointees

00:46:01.178 --> 00:46:06.178
at the FDA as well but the
general public doesn't know.

00:46:06.410 --> 00:46:08.060
And so therefore we need to...

00:46:08.060 --> 00:46:12.120
The fact that I'm seeing
this huge gap in the response

00:46:14.176 --> 00:46:19.090
in terms of serious investment
in vaccine confidence

00:46:19.090 --> 00:46:21.520
and serious investments
in a national education

00:46:21.520 --> 00:46:22.900
and communication campaign.

00:46:22.900 --> 00:46:24.670
Public Health has...

00:46:24.670 --> 00:46:26.210
This is not the first time

00:46:26.210 --> 00:46:27.790
there has been a Public Health response.

00:46:27.790 --> 00:46:30.360
But whenever we have had a major national

00:46:30.360 --> 00:46:31.660
or international level response,

00:46:31.660 --> 00:46:33.450
we've had an evidence-based

00:46:33.450 --> 00:46:35.420
education and communications campaign.

00:46:35.420 --> 00:46:37.850
And a communications
campaign is not run through

00:46:37.850 --> 00:46:41.890
press conferences or tweets exclusively.

00:46:41.890 --> 00:46:44.600
Social media is obviously
a part of all of this stuff

00:46:44.600 --> 00:46:47.000
but it has to be coherent and it has to be

00:46:48.370 --> 00:46:52.210
reasonably evidence-based.

00:46:52.210 --> 00:46:56.500
And so I'll share a piece
of data that we generated

00:46:56.500 --> 00:47:00.730
in collaboration with Alan and Greg Huber

00:47:00.730 --> 00:47:03.970
and a really talented postdoc

00:47:03.970 --> 00:47:06.140
from political science, Scott Bootcamper

00:47:07.120 --> 00:47:11.520
where we had these survey experiments

00:47:11.520 --> 00:47:16.520
where we found that even if
you had approval of the vaccine

00:47:16.710 --> 00:47:20.250
a week before the election
versus in December,

00:47:20.250 --> 00:47:22.280
there would be a much higher acceptance

00:47:22.280 --> 00:47:26.020
and confidence in vaccine if
it were approved in December.

00:47:26.020 --> 00:47:29.170
And then we did this survey experiment

00:47:29.170 --> 00:47:32.360
way before the election, several
weeks before the election.

00:47:32.360 --> 00:47:34.000
And so that is reassuring.

00:47:34.000 --> 00:47:37.330
We also found that elite
endorsement by a Dr. Fauci,

00:47:37.330 --> 00:47:38.470
by Tony Fauci

00:47:38.470 --> 00:47:41.510
but also bipartisan
endorsement could move people.

00:47:41.510 --> 00:47:43.130
So that kind of evidence is there.

00:47:43.130 --> 00:47:45.230
In the context of vaccines,

00:47:45.230 --> 00:47:47.980
I have looked at the impact
of Liberty and priority value.

00:47:47.980 --> 00:47:50.070
So extending some work,
borrowing some work

00:47:50.070 --> 00:47:52.960
from Jonathan Hyatt and
looking at it in the context

00:47:52.960 --> 00:47:54.000
of vaccine decisions

00:47:54.000 --> 00:47:55.910
whether they are moral decisions or not.

00:47:55.910 --> 00:47:59.210
It turns out a lot of these
decisions are moral decisions.

00:47:59.210 --> 00:48:02.130
And some of these, and
I've been looking at

00:48:02.130 --> 00:48:04.480
specifically at liberty
and purity for awhile.

00:48:04.480 --> 00:48:08.070
And because of these authoritarian strains

00:48:08.070 --> 00:48:12.770
that have have become more
prominent in other fields as well

00:48:12.770 --> 00:48:14.280
in terms of pushing back against

00:48:14.280 --> 00:48:16.000
other Public Health interventions,

00:48:16.000 --> 00:48:19.920
we are seeing some of that
work is newly relevant

00:48:20.800 --> 00:48:22.920
in this particular context.

00:48:22.920 --> 00:48:27.040
So I will wrap up by
saying that moving forward,

00:48:27.040 --> 00:48:28.210
there are a lot of things

00:48:28.210 --> 00:48:30.423
that concern us Public health types.

00:48:31.350 --> 00:48:34.720
Governance is at the top of it.

00:48:34.720 --> 00:48:39.210
But also, based on the
composition of the courts,

00:48:39.210 --> 00:48:42.387
one of the things that makes
me nervous is the foundation

00:48:42.387 --> 00:48:44.590
of a lot of public health law is

00:48:44.590 --> 00:48:46.410
this decision that was done around

00:48:46.410 --> 00:48:48.200
smallpox mandatory vaccination,

00:48:48.200 --> 00:48:50.630
Jacobson versus Massachusetts.

00:48:50.630 --> 00:48:55.630
And that sort of explicated
the police powers of the States

00:49:00.060 --> 00:49:02.090
in the modern era or
relatively modern era.

00:49:02.090 --> 00:49:04.450
The decision was of the progressive era

00:49:04.450 --> 00:49:07.200
at the turn of the 20th century.

00:49:07.200 --> 00:49:11.630
And that decision has
spawned a lot of thinking,

00:49:11.630 --> 00:49:16.530
a lot of mainstream, both
legislation and regulation.

00:49:16.530 --> 00:49:18.890
With the new composition of the court,

00:49:18.890 --> 00:49:21.470
I'm not sure that we can take

00:49:21.470 --> 00:49:24.640
those kinds of interventions
for granted, et cetera.

00:49:24.640 --> 00:49:28.940
And so, you know, part of my
work has been on the using

00:49:30.660 --> 00:49:32.760
behaviorally designed legislation

00:49:32.760 --> 00:49:34.460
as a public health intervention.

00:49:34.460 --> 00:49:36.620
And so there are certain things
that we took for granted.

00:49:36.620 --> 00:49:38.190
I don't think we can take it for granted.

00:49:38.190 --> 00:49:41.760
So I'll stop here and I'm sure
we will have the opportunity

00:49:41.760 --> 00:49:43.860
to discuss several things.

00:49:43.860 --> 00:49:46.640
And just to reassure everyone,
this is not a revenge.

00:49:46.640 --> 00:49:49.790
I'm just great crashing
because you're a fun lot.

00:49:49.790 --> 00:49:52.460
- All right, thank you very much Saad Omer

00:49:52.460 --> 00:49:54.210
and thank you to all the panelists.

00:49:54.210 --> 00:49:57.790
So we have a lot of questions
coming in from the audience.

00:49:57.790 --> 00:49:59.720
I just wanted to remind everyone

00:49:59.720 --> 00:50:02.050
that when we conclude the panel discussion

00:50:02.050 --> 00:50:05.550
in about 10 minutes or
so, looking at the clock,

00:50:05.550 --> 00:50:09.380
I'll start with some of the
questions that are being sent in

00:50:09.380 --> 00:50:11.950
to the question and answer function.

00:50:11.950 --> 00:50:15.180
And some of the questions are...

00:50:15.180 --> 00:50:16.760
The questions I've been
trying to read them

00:50:16.760 --> 00:50:19.630
while also paying
attention to my colleagues

00:50:19.630 --> 00:50:22.350
and the questions are outstanding

00:50:22.350 --> 00:50:24.350
and some of them are kind of long.

00:50:24.350 --> 00:50:27.157
So I'm doing my best to sort of remember

00:50:27.157 --> 00:50:30.490
and balance all of this
so good luck to me.

00:50:30.490 --> 00:50:32.860
For now, let me just start
with just a couple of questions

00:50:32.860 --> 00:50:34.950
and then we'll go to question and answer.

00:50:34.950 --> 00:50:38.940
So my first question to the panel is

00:50:38.940 --> 00:50:41.040
there's a difference of
opinion on the panel.

00:50:41.040 --> 00:50:44.420
Maybe people noticed that
there was a little bit

00:50:44.420 --> 00:50:46.820
of a difference of opinion regarding

00:50:46.820 --> 00:50:51.773
how concerning the post-election
period ought to be.

00:50:52.840 --> 00:50:54.830
I think if I remember correctly,

00:50:54.830 --> 00:50:59.550
Isabella, I believe called
today's slow motion coup d'etat.

00:50:59.550 --> 00:51:03.680
And I believe David referred to it as

00:51:04.740 --> 00:51:06.370
the menace has diminished

00:51:06.370 --> 00:51:10.440
and we're now kind of
getting back to normal.

00:51:10.440 --> 00:51:14.685
We've gotten rid of Trump on
the one hand and that's what

00:51:14.685 --> 00:51:19.070
the Democrats were concerned about

00:51:19.070 --> 00:51:23.330
and the election was not
a resounding blue wave

00:51:23.330 --> 00:51:27.180
which was what the
Republicans are worried about.

00:51:27.180 --> 00:51:30.680
So kind of the temperature has gone down

00:51:30.680 --> 00:51:35.680
and we're now entering
a period of more calm

00:51:38.620 --> 00:51:41.860
and more normality, I guess.

00:51:41.860 --> 00:51:43.513
I don't have his exact words.

00:51:44.570 --> 00:51:47.220
So Isabella and David and anyone else who

00:51:47.220 --> 00:51:51.370
wants to enter into the conversation here,

00:51:51.370 --> 00:51:54.893
David, why aren't you more worried?

00:51:56.435 --> 00:52:00.870
And Isabella, could you then respond

00:52:00.870 --> 00:52:04.010
to what David has to say about
why he's not more worried

00:52:04.010 --> 00:52:06.340
by the things that are
clearly worrying you?

00:52:06.340 --> 00:52:09.240
And if anyone else wants
to jump in, of course

00:52:09.240 --> 00:52:12.380
feel free to do so once Isabella and David

00:52:12.380 --> 00:52:16.530
have had a moment to
exchange their views on this.

00:52:17.830 --> 00:52:19.931
(mumbles)

00:52:19.931 --> 00:52:21.480
- I think that

00:52:21.480 --> 00:52:24.030
in order to contest in
an election successfully,

00:52:24.030 --> 00:52:25.590
you need to have some costs to do it,

00:52:25.590 --> 00:52:27.060
some empirical costs to do it

00:52:27.060 --> 00:52:30.320
like the very close
vote in Florida in 2000.

00:52:31.205 --> 00:52:33.979
And that difficulty carried
all the way to the courts

00:52:33.979 --> 00:52:37.090
but it was a 600 vote or
something like that difficulty.

00:52:37.090 --> 00:52:38.506
They had to see what went on

00:52:38.506 --> 00:52:41.494
and they had to look and
fight about what went on.

00:52:41.494 --> 00:52:43.582
Nothing like that is happening this year.

00:52:43.582 --> 00:52:44.923
They're just telling stories.

00:52:44.923 --> 00:52:49.750
Trump people are just telling
stories, that's about it.

00:52:49.750 --> 00:52:53.511
- Yeah, so I agree with
Professor Mayhew that

00:52:53.511 --> 00:52:55.780
clearly this election

00:52:58.250 --> 00:53:03.150
went surprising well
given what was anticipated

00:53:03.150 --> 00:53:05.450
and there was no post electoral violence,

00:53:05.450 --> 00:53:08.900
the sort of very little fraud,
very little irregularities.

00:53:08.900 --> 00:53:13.900
So all this industry worry
about a democratic erosion that

00:53:14.290 --> 00:53:17.723
has in a way been proven wrong.

00:53:18.740 --> 00:53:21.677
My comments referred more to the...

00:53:21.677 --> 00:53:25.010
I linked this sort of right up period more

00:53:25.010 --> 00:53:26.670
to the sort of regular intact

00:53:26.670 --> 00:53:30.043
in a way any conflict that
is happening sort of now.

00:53:31.376 --> 00:53:33.100
So in a way where I come in

00:53:33.100 --> 00:53:34.980
or where sort of where my worry comes in

00:53:34.980 --> 00:53:36.270
is more having survived.

00:53:36.270 --> 00:53:40.100
And again, thinking about
the effort to legitimize.

00:53:40.100 --> 00:53:45.100
of course, President Biden is
gonna be our next president.

00:53:46.360 --> 00:53:49.900
Okay, but still, if you
look at the service today,

00:53:49.900 --> 00:53:54.900
30% or more of voters
still, rightly believe

00:53:55.100 --> 00:53:58.613
that this is not a legitimate president.

00:54:00.350 --> 00:54:03.843
Because kind of his whole
strategies that have been

00:54:03.843 --> 00:54:06.590
sort of that Trump has
used during the campaign

00:54:06.590 --> 00:54:09.210
about sort of invoking fraud

00:54:09.210 --> 00:54:11.960
have resonated with some voters.

00:54:11.960 --> 00:54:14.600
So the concern here is more about

00:54:17.160 --> 00:54:22.160
shrugging off this illegitimacy
kind of image in a way.

00:54:24.569 --> 00:54:26.360
And sort of, again if we look at sort of

00:54:26.360 --> 00:54:29.110
the book by Nancy Bermeo (mumbles)

00:54:29.110 --> 00:54:31.639
everything you know and
compare it to politics, I mean

00:54:31.639 --> 00:54:33.910
and I don't like to use this
comparison, Weimar Germany.

00:54:33.910 --> 00:54:36.168
But in Weimar Germany,
the problem was that

00:54:36.168 --> 00:54:39.160
a significant part of the
population never thought

00:54:39.160 --> 00:54:41.160
that democracy was legitimate

00:54:41.160 --> 00:54:44.040
and that sort of it was never possible

00:54:44.040 --> 00:54:46.350
for the democratic kind of group

00:54:46.350 --> 00:54:49.730
in the society at that
time to kind of shrug off

00:54:49.730 --> 00:54:52.200
that sort of red image.

00:54:52.200 --> 00:54:55.437
So this is a very risky strategy.

00:54:55.437 --> 00:54:58.020
One sort of writes that
this is on the agenda

00:54:58.020 --> 00:54:59.960
and one sort of ratified about this

00:54:59.960 --> 00:55:02.640
and the answer sort read
based on what we noticed

00:55:02.640 --> 00:55:07.640
is sometimes it's just not
possible and it doesn't end well.

00:55:08.360 --> 00:55:12.560
So I think we are commenting
on different parts

00:55:13.940 --> 00:55:17.382
of the sort of the election.

00:55:17.382 --> 00:55:19.833
And through right the
distance sort of like

00:55:19.833 --> 00:55:23.333
what's going to happen
sort of looking forward.

00:55:24.240 --> 00:55:29.240
Instead of kind of when they
settle with these voters.

00:55:30.901 --> 00:55:33.122
So I think this is where
the disagreement is.

00:55:33.122 --> 00:55:34.370
(mumbles)

