- Yeah. I agree with you, Jacob
about staying off of Twitter

01:25:00.840 --> 01:25:03.450
although I do have to say and I think that

01:25:05.120 --> 01:25:09.580
as not certainly one of the
armchair epidemiologists

01:25:09.580 --> 01:25:13.040
that Saad railed against, I
definitely feel that getting

01:25:13.040 --> 01:25:16.990
into epi Twitter made
me epically more aware

01:25:16.990 --> 01:25:21.990
of the oncoming pandemic
back in February and March

01:25:22.920 --> 01:25:25.150
then probably it would
have been elsewhere.

01:25:25.150 --> 01:25:26.680
So I must say that there is

01:25:26.680 --> 01:25:29.520
an incredible information
campaign that is happening

01:25:29.520 --> 01:25:33.920
by epidemiologists to their
absolute credit on Twitter.

01:25:33.920 --> 01:25:37.260
There's a lot of work that has been done

01:25:38.360 --> 01:25:41.610
to accumulate data and
to accumulate knowledge

01:25:41.610 --> 01:25:42.690
in really readable form.

01:25:42.690 --> 01:25:47.196
So I agree with you that
Twitter is by and large bad

01:25:47.196 --> 01:25:51.580
for your blood pressure, I think.

01:25:51.580 --> 01:25:56.420
But for information that you might not

01:25:56.420 --> 01:25:57.560
easily come in contact with,

01:25:57.560 --> 01:26:00.410
I think getting into one
of those circles is great.

01:26:00.410 --> 01:26:05.410
In terms of books, Will
Howell and Terry Moe's

01:26:05.770 --> 01:26:08.390
newest book on the presidency

01:26:08.390 --> 01:26:12.870
I found fascinating and
infuriating at the same time

01:26:12.870 --> 01:26:15.239
mostly because it's about
populist presidencies

01:26:15.239 --> 01:26:18.267
and I think I would recommend that 100%.

01:26:19.580 --> 01:26:21.240
- Great, thank you, Christina.

01:26:21.240 --> 01:26:22.450
Isabella?

01:26:22.450 --> 01:26:25.940
- Yeah, so I thank you for this question.

01:26:25.940 --> 01:26:27.660
It's actually very thought-provoking.

01:26:27.660 --> 01:26:29.220
And so for me,

01:26:29.220 --> 01:26:32.950
I have been reading a lot of
historical books on pandemics

01:26:34.290 --> 01:26:35.990
just trying to understand that.

01:26:35.990 --> 01:26:39.850
And one that I would strongly recommend is

01:26:39.850 --> 01:26:43.920
by a historian at UCLA,
his name is Peter Baldwin

01:26:43.920 --> 01:26:45.960
and the book is called
"Contagion and the State"

01:26:45.960 --> 01:26:48.510
and it is about sort
of historical responses

01:26:48.510 --> 01:26:49.953
to health crisis.

01:26:50.910 --> 01:26:53.589
So I would add that to the list

01:26:53.589 --> 01:26:56.610
of sort of also Nancy
Bermejo, who is an author

01:26:56.610 --> 01:26:58.950
I find sort of highly relevant today.

01:26:58.950 --> 01:27:01.130
I mentioned about in the beginning.

01:27:01.130 --> 01:27:02.650
- Thank you very much, Isabella.

01:27:02.650 --> 01:27:03.650
David?

01:27:03.650 --> 01:27:05.977
- I'll go to Jacobs already
mentioned Jim Scottsbluff

01:27:05.977 --> 01:27:08.750
and I agree with that in
a book something like that

01:27:08.750 --> 01:27:10.910
which I've recommended to you, by the way.

01:27:10.910 --> 01:27:12.933
It's by Walter Scheidel.

01:27:12.933 --> 01:27:16.020
He's an Economic History
professor at Stanford.

01:27:16.020 --> 01:27:17.390
I can't remember the exact title

01:27:17.390 --> 01:27:20.900
but I think it has equality
in it or inequality.

01:27:20.900 --> 01:27:23.270
It also has violence in it.

01:27:23.270 --> 01:27:25.380
It's about equality and inequality

01:27:25.380 --> 01:27:27.423
during the last 3000 years.

01:27:28.820 --> 01:27:29.990
- Great, thank you, David.

01:27:29.990 --> 01:27:31.053
And Saad?

01:27:32.150 --> 01:27:34.710
- So I would recommend and
I've been reading a bit

01:27:34.710 --> 01:27:37.130
not as much as I would have liked to

01:27:37.130 --> 01:27:40.700
because of some of the
other stuff that have to do.

01:27:40.700 --> 01:27:45.140
But I would say very early on,

01:27:45.140 --> 01:27:49.750
I went back to Camus "The Plague."

01:27:49.750 --> 01:27:53.263
It's a classic and it's, you know,

01:27:56.080 --> 01:27:59.280
I'm not exaggerating but it
almost gave me goosebumps

01:27:59.280 --> 01:28:01.700
in terms of earlier on.

01:28:01.700 --> 01:28:04.290
Like how the more things change,

01:28:04.290 --> 01:28:07.070
the more they remain the same, et cetera.

01:28:07.070 --> 01:28:09.440
So I would suggest going back to that.

01:28:09.440 --> 01:28:12.090
the Atlantic has had
some of the best writing

01:28:12.090 --> 01:28:14.040
on the pandemic, I absolutely agree.

01:28:14.040 --> 01:28:17.740
Especially Ed Yong's on it.

01:28:17.740 --> 01:28:19.060
It's very thoughtful.

01:28:19.060 --> 01:28:23.250
It's not at all superficial.

01:28:23.250 --> 01:28:24.420
And the last thing I would say,

01:28:24.420 --> 01:28:26.360
if you care about institutions,

01:28:26.360 --> 01:28:31.260
read the long form article
by Pro-Publica on CDCs

01:28:31.260 --> 01:28:32.550
brawl on this.

01:28:32.550 --> 01:28:35.560
Take the time, read it
and then read it again

01:28:36.530 --> 01:28:40.100
because there are lessons for
other institutions as well.

01:28:40.100 --> 01:28:41.900
So I'll pause here.

01:28:41.900 --> 01:28:43.160
- Thank you very much.

01:28:43.160 --> 01:28:46.140
Thank you all so much for taking the time

01:28:47.900 --> 01:28:51.453
to join together this evening
for this conversation.

01:28:52.360 --> 01:28:54.970
I hope everyone in the
audience appreciated it

01:28:54.970 --> 01:28:56.870
as much as I did.

01:28:56.870 --> 01:28:59.880
I'd like to once again advertise,

01:28:59.880 --> 01:29:02.770
we're gonna do this again
a slightly different way.

01:29:02.770 --> 01:29:05.930
In two weeks, we're having a session.

01:29:05.930 --> 01:29:09.190
What did voters do and why did they do it?

01:29:09.190 --> 01:29:13.280
Another panel discussion
sponsored by ISPS.

01:29:13.280 --> 01:29:16.500
With that, I'd like to,
again, thank the panelists.

01:29:16.500 --> 01:29:19.800
Thank Pam Greene and Lamar Pier

01:29:19.800 --> 01:29:21.990
who worked to set up today's event

01:29:21.990 --> 01:29:23.810
and thank you all for joining us.

01:29:23.810 --> 01:29:27.730
I look forward to seeing
you at future ISPS events.

01:29:27.730 --> 01:29:29.120
- Thank you, Alan.

01:29:29.120 --> 01:29:30.164
- Thank you.
- Of course.

01:29:30.164 --> 01:29:31.465
Bye bye everyone.

01:29:31.465 --> 01:29:32.298
